{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>For it is not a tree of food.</b> Even if it was a fruit tree but has dried up, you are permitted to cut it down. Another interpretation: one must emend the verse — 'for the tree of the field to come before you in the siege' — and is it 'in the place'? Rather, this is what it says: you shall not cut down any fruit tree that is in the field, except that tree through which a man in the siege can flee from before you and enter it, to stand against you and prevent you from coming to the wall — that is, the forest adjacent to the besieged city — that you may cut down, even if they are fruit trees." "<b>Only a tree which you know is not a tree of food</b> etc. Even if it is far from the city. And why is this stated? If he has permission to cut down a fruit tree, a tree that is not a fruit tree — all the more so! Rather, it writes for you 'only a tree which you know' etc., to teach you that we do not punish from inference." "Another interpretation: rather, the trees of the field that are far from the city, through which a man can come from before you to take refuge in the siege — that is, the trees of the forest from which the people of the city make structures around the city, in order to come from before you and enter and take refuge in them in the siege — even if they are fruit trees. <b>Only a tree which you know</b> etc. — even if it is adjacent to the city. <b>It you may destroy and cut down</b> — to include everything. If its conclusion is to include everything, what does the verse teach by saying 'every tree of food'? To tell you that a barren tree takes precedence over a fruit tree. One might think even if it is superior in value — the verse says <b>only</b>. Another interpretation: as its plain meaning — and that you shall not cut down. And why? 'For the man is the tree of the field' — for the strength of the man besieging the city, and his life, is the tree of the field. An analogy: 'for the man is a besieger' — its meaning is 'for the life of the man is a besieger,' for by eating from it he is able to stand there. 'To come before you in the siege' — in order to bring and bring in the people of the city from before you in the siege. <b>Until it falls.</b>"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {2 "The word מעולה in the phrase אפילו מעולה בדמים is translated literally as 'superior in value,' following the standard reading where 'only' (רק) limits cutting barren trees to cases where they are not more valuable than fruit trees. The phrase 'כי נפש הוא חובל' is translated hyper-literally as 'for the man is a besieger' per the instruction to translate obscure passages literally and record the difficulty; the text as given is textually problematic and the commentary's own paraphrase immediately follows."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:53:39.716461385Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}